%% ============================================================================
%% Small-Molecule Drug Discovery Platform
%% EML — complete model: ERD · business rules · workflows
%%
%% Every rule, state and saga diagram below is stock Mermaid and renders as
%% drawn. The ERD carries EML's OPTIONAL modifier, which Mermaid's ER grammar
%% has no equivalent for, so it renders through toRenderableMermaid()
%% (packages/web/src/lib/mermaid-render.ts) — the normalization the application
%% applies to every ERD before handing it to Mermaid.
%%
%% Scope: a discovery portfolio from target to candidate, and the laboratory
%% that feeds it —
%%   portfolio    Program, Target, CompoundSeries
%%   chemistry    Compound
%%   screening    Assay, AssayRun, AssayResult, AdmetProfile
%%   laboratory   Experiment, Sample, InventoryTransaction,
%%                Instrument, InstrumentBooking
%%   outsourcing  Vendor, VendorRequest
%%   quality      DeviationReport, CAPA
%%   people       User, Team
%%
%% Generate:
%%   bun run generate:tanstack -- -i examples/drug-discovery.eml.mmd \
%%     -o generated-projects/drug-discovery -n drug-discovery
%%
%% Check:
%%   bun language/checker.ts examples/drug-discovery.eml.mmd
%%
%% ---- What this model is about ----------------------------------------------
%% A discovery programme is a bet on a biological target. The bet is tested by
%% making compounds and measuring them, and the measuring is the expensive part,
%% so the shape of the data is: a Program aims at a Target; chemists make
%% Compounds grouped into a CompoundSeries; biologists define an Assay and run
%% it (AssayRun) to produce an AssayResult per compound; and DMPK scientists
%% build an AdmetProfile that decides whether a potent compound is also a drug.
%%
%% Potency alone never advanced a molecule. That is why AdmetProfile is a first
%% class entity here rather than a few columns on Compound: solubility,
%% permeability, metabolic stability, hERG liability and protein binding are
%% each measured separately, at different times, by different people, and a
%% compound can die on any one of them.
%%
%% ---- How the three layers fit together --------------------------------------
%% Structure is the ERD in Section 1. Everything after it is behaviour, and the
%% file is arranged so behaviour reads in the order it fires:
%%
%%   rules (2-4)      declarative decisions — the gate an experiment passes
%%                    before it runs, how a deviation is graded, whether an
%%                    instrument may be booked. Compiled to GoRules decision
%%                    graphs and evaluated around the write.
%%   state (5-7)      the lifecycle a programme, an experiment and a corrective
%%                    action move through, and who may move them.
%%   hooks (8-10)     imperative side effects bound to CRUD lifecycle events.
%%   sagas (11-13)    multi-step, multi-entity processes, one per trigger kind:
%%                    a rule names the first, the second runs on every update,
%%                    the third runs on delete.
%%
%% The three sagas are one per trigger kind on purpose. It is the difference
%% between "escalate when the deviation is critical", "write a ledger row every
%% time a sample moves", and "clear the runs before the assay goes".
%%
%% Reporting: the questions written for each role by hand sit beside charted reports derived from the
%% model's own structure (its lifecycles, enumerated columns, dates, flags, measures, parents and optional
%% fields), and a %%dashboard per job groups them. Every role can run at least 50 charted reports and has
%% at least 10 dashboards of three or more.
%% ============================================================================

