    %% ---- Portfolio ----------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Program help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. Everything else in this application ultimately hangs off a programme, and a programme that is discontinued takes its series and experiments out of the active portfolio with it.
    %%field Program.program_code help: The short internal code the programme is known by in every meeting and slide, such as ONC-114.
    %%field Program.name help: The readable name of the programme.
    %%field Program.therapeutic_area help: The broad disease area the programme sits in. Portfolio reviews are usually run one area at a time.
    %%field Program.indication help: The specific disease the medicine is intended to treat.
    %%field Program.stage help: Where the programme has reached, from picking a target to selecting a clinical candidate. Stage decides which data is expected to exist.
    %%field Program.status help: Whether the programme is active, paused, finished or stopped.
    %%field Program.target_id help: The biological target this programme acts on. One target per programme, by design — two targets is two programmes.
    %%field Program.owner_id help: The scientist accountable for the programme's science.
    %%field Program.started_on help: The date the programme was funded and work began.
    %%field Program.target_candidate_date help: The date the programme aims to nominate a clinical candidate. A plan, not a promise.
    %%field Program.annual_budget help: Approved spend for the current year, in the site's reporting currency.
    %%field Program.objective help: What the programme is trying to prove, in a sentence a new joiner could read.

    %%entity Target help: A protein or gene the programme intends to act on. The validation level is the most important thing on the record: a target nobody has validated in vivo is a much larger bet than one with clinical precedent, and the whole programme inherits that risk.
    %%field Target.gene_symbol help: The HGNC gene symbol, such as EGFR or BTK.
    %%field Target.uniprot_accession help: The UniProt accession for the protein, which is how this target is matched against external data.
    %%field Target.name help: The full protein name.
    %%field Target.target_class help: The structural family the target belongs to. It largely decides which screening approaches are worth trying.
    %%field Target.validation_level help: How strong the evidence is that acting on this target changes the disease — from a hypothesis to clinical precedent.
    %%field Target.is_antitarget help: Set when the target is one to avoid rather than to hit, such as hERG. Selectivity panels are built from antitargets.
    %%field Target.biological_rationale help: The argument for why this target matters in this disease.

    %%entity CompoundSeries help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. Medicinal chemistry advances by series rather than by molecule, so this is the unit a portfolio review actually discusses — a series is progressed, deprioritised or abandoned as a whole.
    %%field CompoundSeries.series_code help: The short code the series is known by.
    %%field CompoundSeries.name help: A readable name for the series, usually naming the scaffold.
    %%field CompoundSeries.program_id help: The programme this series belongs to.
    %%field CompoundSeries.scaffold_smiles help: The core structure shared by the series, as SMILES.
    %%field CompoundSeries.status help: Whether the series is being actively worked, parked or dropped.
    %%field CompoundSeries.compound_count help: How many compounds have been made in this series so far.
    %%field CompoundSeries.design_hypothesis help: What the chemists believe this scaffold can be made to do, and what they are changing to find out.

    %% ---- Chemistry ----------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Compound help: One registered chemical structure. A compound is registered once and measured many times; its calculated properties are computed on registration from the structure, while everything measured about it lives in AssayResult and AdmetProfile.
    %%field Compound.compound_number help: The internal registration number, the identifier the compound is known by everywhere else.
    %%field Compound.smiles help: The chemical structure in SMILES notation. This is the definition of the compound; everything else is derived from it or measured about it.
    %%field Compound.inchi_key help: The hashed structure identifier, computed on registration. It is what makes duplicate registration detectable.
    %%field Compound.common_name help: A human name for the compound where it has one.
    %%field Compound.cas_number help: The CAS registry number, where the compound is a known substance.
    %%field Compound.formula help: The molecular formula, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.compound_class help: What kind of molecule this is. Most discovery compounds are small molecules; fragments are handled differently because they are deliberately weak.
    %%field Compound.compound_series_id help: The chemical series this compound belongs to, where it belongs to one.
    %%field Compound.molecular_weight help: Molecular weight in daltons, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.logp help: Calculated lipophilicity. High logP buys potency and costs solubility, which is the oldest trade in medicinal chemistry.
    %%field Compound.tpsa help: Topological polar surface area, in square angstroms. It predicts whether a molecule can cross a membrane.
    %%field Compound.hydrogen_bond_donors help: Count of hydrogen bond donors, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.hydrogen_bond_acceptors help: Count of hydrogen bond acceptors, computed from the structure.
    %%field Compound.rotatable_bonds help: Count of rotatable bonds. More flexibility usually means a worse binder and worse oral absorption.
    %%field Compound.lipinski_violations help: How many of Lipinski's four rule-of-five criteria the compound breaks. Two or more is a warning rather than a verdict.
    %%field Compound.registration_status help: Where the compound is in registration. Only an approved compound may be screened.
    %%field Compound.registered_by_id help: The chemist who registered the structure.

    %% ---- Screening ----------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Assay help: A defined way of measuring something about a compound. The definition is separate from the running of it because the same assay is run hundreds of times, and a result is only comparable with another when both came from the same assay definition.
    %%field Assay.assay_code help: The short code the assay is known by on plates and in reports.
    %%field Assay.name help: A readable description of what the assay measures.
    %%field Assay.assay_type help: Whether this is a purified-protein assay, a cell assay, a biophysical measurement, an ADMET endpoint, a selectivity panel or an animal study.
    %%field Assay.readout help: What the assay reports — a potency value, a percentage inhibition, a half-life, a permeability.
    %%field Assay.unit help: The unit the readout is expressed in. A number without its unit is not a result.
    %%field Assay.target_id help: The target this assay measures activity against, where it measures one.
    %%field Assay.protocol_reference help: A reference to the written protocol the assay follows.
    %%field Assay.acceptance_z_prime help: The lowest Z-prime a run of this assay may have and still be trusted. Below it, the plate is noise.
    %%field Assay.is_active help: Whether the assay is still available to be run.

    %%entity AssayRun help: One execution of an assay — typically one plate, on one instrument, on one day, by one operator. The run is where quality is judged: if its Z-prime is below the assay's acceptance threshold, every result on the plate is suspect no matter how good the numbers look.
    %%field AssayRun.run_reference help: The identifier for this run, used to trace a result back to the day and plate it came from.
    %%field AssayRun.assay_id help: The assay definition being executed.
    %%field AssayRun.experiment_id help: The experiment this run was scheduled under, where it was scheduled under one.
    %%field AssayRun.instrument_id help: The instrument that read the plate.
    %%field AssayRun.operated_by_id help: The person who ran the plate.
    %%field AssayRun.run_date help: The date the plate was run.
    %%field AssayRun.plate_barcode help: The barcode of the physical plate, which is how the run is matched to the raw instrument file.
    %%field AssayRun.z_prime help: The statistical quality of the plate, between minus infinity and 1. Above 0.5 is a good assay; below the assay's threshold the run is rejected.
    %%field AssayRun.qc_status help: Whether the run has passed quality control. Results from a failed run must not be used to make decisions.
    %%field AssayRun.status help: Where the run has got to, from scheduled to completed or failed.
    %%field AssayRun.retest_count help: How many times this run has been retested after a failure. A plate that keeps failing is an assay problem, not a compound problem.
    %%field AssayRun.failure_reason help: Why the run failed, where it did.

    %%entity AssayResult help: One compound's measurement from one run. This is the row the whole application exists to produce: a number, its unit, and enough context to know whether to believe it.
    %%entity AssayResult parent: AssayRun
    %%field AssayResult.assay_run_id help: The run this measurement came from.
    %%field AssayResult.compound_id help: The compound that was measured.
    %%field AssayResult.value help: The measured number, in the unit beside it.
    %%field AssayResult.unit help: The unit of the measurement. Kept on the result as well as the assay because units are occasionally reported differently for the same assay.
    %%field AssayResult.qualifier help: Whether the value is exact or a bound. A compound too weak to measure gives a greater-than, and treating that as an exact number is a classic way to mislead a project.
    %%field AssayResult.replicate_count help: How many replicates the value is averaged over.
    %%field AssayResult.standard_deviation help: The spread across replicates, which is how much of the value to trust.
    %%field AssayResult.is_validated help: Whether a scientist has reviewed and accepted this result.

    %%entity AdmetProfile help: What is known about whether a compound could behave like a drug — absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion and toxicity. Kept apart from Compound because each endpoint is measured separately, at different times, by different people, and a potent compound can be killed by any one of them.
    %%entity AdmetProfile parent: Compound
    %%field AdmetProfile.compound_id help: The compound this profile describes.
    %%field AdmetProfile.kinetic_solubility_um help: How much of the compound will dissolve, in micromolar. A compound that will not dissolve cannot be dosed and often cannot even be assayed honestly.
    %%field AdmetProfile.caco2_permeability help: Apparent permeability through a Caco-2 cell monolayer, the standard laboratory stand-in for absorption across the gut wall.
    %%field AdmetProfile.microsomal_half_life_min help: How long the compound survives in liver microsomes, in minutes. Short half-lives mean the liver clears the drug before it can work.
    %%field AdmetProfile.herg_ic50_um help: Potency against the hERG cardiac ion channel. Here a high number is the good outcome: hERG is an antitarget, and blocking it causes arrhythmia.
    %%field AdmetProfile.cyp3a4_inhibition_percent help: How much the compound inhibits CYP3A4, the enzyme that metabolises much of the pharmacopoeia. Strong inhibition means drug-drug interactions.
    %%field AdmetProfile.plasma_protein_bound_percent help: The percentage bound to plasma proteins. Only the unbound fraction is free to act, so a highly bound compound needs a much higher dose.
    %%field AdmetProfile.is_bbb_penetrant help: Whether the compound crosses the blood-brain barrier. Required for a neuroscience programme and usually unwanted everywhere else.
    %%field AdmetProfile.ames_result help: The outcome of the Ames mutagenicity test. A positive is normally the end of the compound.
    %%field AdmetProfile.assessed_on help: The date this profile was compiled.
    %%field AdmetProfile.assessed_by_id help: The DMPK scientist who compiled it.

    %% ---- Laboratory ---------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity Experiment help: A planned piece of laboratory work, reviewed and approved before it runs. The experiment is what instrument bookings, outsourced requests, samples and deviations all hang from, so it is the record an auditor follows to reconstruct what was done.
    %%field Experiment.title help: What the experiment is called.
    %%field Experiment.status help: Where the experiment has reached in its lifecycle, from draft to completed.
    %%field Experiment.program_id help: The programme the work is being done for.
    %%field Experiment.pi_id help: The principal investigator accountable for the experiment.
    %%field Experiment.project_code help: The code the work is charged to.
    %%field Experiment.estimated_duration_days help: How many days the work is expected to take. Required before the experiment may be submitted.
    %%field Experiment.is_gmp_critical help: Whether the work falls under good manufacturing practice. It raises the bar on every deviation raised against it.
    %%field Experiment.submitted_at help: When the experiment was submitted for review.
    %%field Experiment.completed_at help: When the work finished.
    %%field Experiment.objective help: What the experiment is meant to establish.

    %%entity Sample help: A physical quantity of material in a tube, plate or vial, in a known place. A sample is the thing a scientist can actually pick up, and the difference between the compound and the sample is the difference between a structure and eight microlitres of it in freezer A.
    %%field Sample.sample_barcode help: The barcode on the physical container. Scanning it is how the material is identified at the bench.
    %%field Sample.compound_id help: The compound this material is, where it is a registered compound.
    %%field Sample.experiment_id help: The experiment the sample was prepared for.
    %%field Sample.volume_ul help: Volume on hand, in microlitres.
    %%field Sample.mass_mg help: Mass on hand, in milligrams.
    %%field Sample.concentration_mm help: Concentration of the solution, in millimolar.
    %%field Sample.physical_state help: What form the material is in, which decides how it can be handled.
    %%field Sample.storage_location help: Where the sample physically is — the freezer, shelf and rack.
    %%field Sample.storage_temperature help: The temperature the sample must be kept at.
    %%field Sample.expiration_date help: The date after which the material should not be trusted.
    %%field Sample.status help: Whether the sample is available, spoken for, used up or quarantined.

    %%entity InventoryTransaction help: One movement of material — received, split into aliquots, consumed, moved, discarded or corrected. Samples are edited constantly, and a stock figure nobody can explain is worse than none, so every change to a sample leaves one of these behind.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.sample_id help: The sample that moved.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.performed_by_id help: Who moved it. Empty when the movement was recorded automatically by the disposition process rather than by a person at the bench.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.transaction_type help: What kind of movement this was.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.quantity_change help: How much material was added or removed. Negative for a consumption.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.from_location help: Where the material was before.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.to_location help: Where it went.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.occurred_at help: When the movement happened, where that is known separately from when the row was written.
    %%field InventoryTransaction.reason help: Why, where the movement needs explaining.

    %%entity Instrument help: A piece of laboratory equipment that produces data. Calibration is the field that matters: an overdue instrument invalidates everything measured on it, which is why a booking is refused rather than warned about.
    %%field Instrument.asset_tag help: The asset tag physically stuck to the instrument.
    %%field Instrument.name help: What people call it.
    %%field Instrument.instrument_type help: What kind of instrument it is.
    %%field Instrument.calibration_status help: Whether calibration is current, approaching, overdue or the instrument is out of service.
    %%field Instrument.last_calibration_date help: When it was last calibrated.
    %%field Instrument.next_calibration_date help: When calibration falls due.
    %%field Instrument.location help: Which laboratory it lives in.
    %%field Instrument.is_active help: Whether the instrument is in service. An inactive instrument cannot be booked.

    %%entity InstrumentBooking help: A reserved block of time on an instrument. Instruments are the scarce resource in most laboratories, so the booking is where contention is resolved and where an overdue calibration stops work before it starts.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.instrument_id help: The instrument being booked.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.experiment_id help: The experiment the time is for.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.booked_by_id help: Who made the booking.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.start_time help: When the block starts.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.end_time help: When it ends.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.status help: Whether the booking is pending, confirmed, running, finished or cancelled.
    %%field InstrumentBooking.cancellation_reason help: Why the booking was cancelled, where it was.

    %% ---- Quality and suppliers ----------------------------------------------
    %%entity Vendor help: An external supplier or contract research organisation. Much of discovery chemistry and biology is outsourced, so a vendor's approval status and service tier decide what work may be sent where.
    %%field Vendor.name help: The organisation's name.
    %%field Vendor.vendor_type help: What the vendor does for you.
    %%field Vendor.sla_tier help: The service level agreed with them, which sets the turnaround you can rely on.
    %%field Vendor.contact_email help: The address work is arranged through.
    %%field Vendor.contact_phone help: A number for when email is too slow.
    %%field Vendor.country help: Where the vendor operates, which affects shipping and what may be sent.
    %%field Vendor.is_approved help: Whether the vendor has passed qualification. Unapproved vendors may not be sent work.

    %%entity VendorRequest help: A piece of work sent outside — a synthesis, a panel, an analysis. It is raised against an experiment so that outsourced results can be traced back to the work that needed them.
    %%field VendorRequest.request_number help: The reference the request is tracked by with the vendor.
    %%field VendorRequest.vendor_id help: Who the work was sent to.
    %%field VendorRequest.experiment_id help: The experiment the work belongs to.
    %%field VendorRequest.raised_by_id help: Who raised the request.
    %%field VendorRequest.description help: What was asked for.
    %%field VendorRequest.quoted_cost help: The price quoted for the work.
    %%field VendorRequest.required_date help: The date the results are needed.
    %%field VendorRequest.status help: Where the request has got to.

    %%entity DeviationReport help: A record that something did not go to plan. Deviations are the raw material of laboratory quality: severity is assigned by rule rather than by opinion, and a critical one opens a corrective action automatically.
    %%field DeviationReport.experiment_id help: The experiment the deviation happened in.
    %%field DeviationReport.reported_by_id help: Who reported it.
    %%field DeviationReport.severity help: How serious it is. Assigned by the routing rule rather than chosen by the reporter.
    %%field DeviationReport.status help: Where the investigation has reached.
    %%field DeviationReport.anomaly_description help: What happened, in enough detail for somebody who was not there.
    %%field DeviationReport.anomaly_timestamp help: When it happened, as distinct from when it was reported.
    %%field DeviationReport.instrument_failure_involved help: Whether equipment was implicated, which raises the severity and usually the scope.

    %%entity CAPA help: A corrective and preventive action — what was done about a deviation, and what stops it happening again. A CAPA is closed only when its effectiveness has been verified, which is the whole difference between fixing an incident and fixing a process.
    %%field CAPA.deviation_report_id help: The deviation this action answers.
    %%field CAPA.remediation_owner_id help: Who owns getting it done.
    %%field CAPA.title help: A short description of the action.
    %%field CAPA.root_cause_category help: What the investigation concluded was the underlying cause.
    %%field CAPA.status help: Where the action has reached. Closing requires verification.
    %%field CAPA.required_resolution_date help: The date the action must be complete by, set from the severity.
    %%field CAPA.effectiveness_metric help: The measure used to judge whether the fix worked.
    %%field CAPA.remediation_plan help: What is actually going to be done.

    %% ---- People -------------------------------------------------------------
    %%entity User help: Somebody who works in the laboratory or on the portfolio. The role is what decides which of the entities above they can see at all.
    %%field User.email help: Their work address, which is also how they sign in.
    %%field User.first_name help: Given name.
    %%field User.last_name help: Family name.
    %%field User.role help: Their function, which determines what this application shows them.
    %%field User.team_id help: The team they belong to.
    %%field User.department help: The department they sit in.
    %%field User.is_active help: Whether they still work here.
    %%field User.last_login help: When they last signed in.

    %%entity Team help: A group of people who work together, usually around a technique or a programme.
    %%field Team.name help: What the team is called.
    %%field Team.manager_id help: Who runs it.
    %%field Team.department help: The department the team belongs to.

    %% Form layout: headed sections of two-column rows (`|` between rows,
    %% `,` within one; a row of one spans the full width).
    %%section entity: Program; name: Program; help: Which discovery program this is, the disease it aims at, and who is accountable for it.; rows: name, program_code | therapeutic_area, indication | owner_id, target_id
    %%section entity: Program; name: Progress; help: How far the program has got and whether it is still funded to continue.; rows: stage | started_on, target_candidate_date
    %%section entity: Program; name: Mandate; help: What the program was funded to achieve and what it may spend a year doing it.; rows: annual_budget | objective
    %%section entity: Target; name: Target; help: The protein the program acts on, as the gene and UniProt entry identify it.; rows: name, gene_symbol | uniprot_accession, target_class
    %%section entity: Target; name: Validation; help: How strongly the target is tied to the disease, and whether hitting it is something to avoid.; rows: validation_level, is_antitarget | biological_rationale
    %%section entity: CompoundSeries; name: Series; help: The chemical series, the program it belongs to, and whether chemists are still making it.; rows: name, series_code | program_id, status
    %%section entity: CompoundSeries; name: Chemistry; help: The core structure the series is built around and the idea the chemists are testing with it.; rows: scaffold_smiles, compound_count | design_hypothesis
    %%section entity: Compound; name: Registration; help: The compound's registry number, the series it was made in, and whether registration is complete.; rows: compound_number, registration_status | compound_series_id, registered_by_id | common_name, compound_class
    %%section entity: Compound; name: Structure; help: The identifiers chemistry systems match the molecule on — any two compounds sharing an InChIKey are the same compound.; rows: smiles | inchi_key, cas_number | formula
    %%section entity: Compound; name: Properties; help: The calculated physicochemical profile that decides whether the molecule looks like a drug.; rows: molecular_weight, logp | tpsa, rotatable_bonds | hydrogen_bond_donors, hydrogen_bond_acceptors | lipinski_violations
    %%section entity: Assay; name: Assay; help: The test, what it measures, against which target, and whether it is still run.; rows: name, assay_code | assay_type, target_id | is_active
    %%section entity: Assay; name: Method; help: What the assay reads out, in which unit, the protocol it follows, and the Z-prime a run must reach to count.; rows: readout, unit | protocol_reference, acceptance_z_prime
    %%section entity: AssayRun; name: Run; help: One execution of an assay — which plate, which experiment, on which instrument, by whom.; rows: run_reference, assay_id | experiment_id, instrument_id | operated_by_id, run_date | plate_barcode
    %%section entity: AssayRun; name: Quality; help: Whether the run met its quality bar; results from a failed run are not used for decisions.; rows: z_prime, qc_status | status, retest_count | failure_reason
    %%section entity: AssayResult; name: Result; help: One compound's measured value in the run, with how it was qualified and how reproducible it was.; rows: assay_run_id, compound_id | value, unit | qualifier, is_validated | replicate_count, standard_deviation
    %%section entity: AdmetProfile; name: Assessment; help: Which compound was profiled, when, and by whom.; rows: compound_id | assessed_on, assessed_by_id
    %%section entity: AdmetProfile; name: Absorption and metabolism; help: How well the compound dissolves, crosses membranes and survives the liver.; rows: kinetic_solubility_um, caco2_permeability | microsomal_half_life_min, plasma_protein_bound_percent | is_bbb_penetrant
    %%section entity: AdmetProfile; name: Safety signals; help: The early toxicity flags that stop a compound — cardiac channel, drug-interaction and mutagenicity results.; rows: herg_ic50_um, cyp3a4_inhibition_percent | ames_result
    %%section entity: Experiment; name: Experiment; help: What is being done, for which program, under which principal investigator, and where it stands.; rows: title | program_id, pi_id | project_code | objective
    %%section entity: Experiment; name: Schedule and compliance; help: How long it should take, when it was submitted and finished, and whether GMP rules apply.; rows: estimated_duration_days, is_gmp_critical | submitted_at, completed_at
    %%section entity: Sample; name: Sample; help: The physical tube, which compound is in it, and the experiment it was drawn for.; rows: sample_barcode, status | compound_id, experiment_id
    %%section entity: Sample; name: Contents; help: How much material is in the tube and in what state — what the next request can draw on.; rows: volume_ul, mass_mg | concentration_mm, physical_state
    %%section entity: Sample; name: Storage; help: Where the tube is kept, how cold, and the day it must no longer be used.; rows: storage_location, storage_temperature | expiration_date
    %%section entity: InventoryTransaction; name: Movement; help: One change to a sample's stock — what moved, how much, from where to where, and who did it.; rows: sample_id, transaction_type | quantity_change, occurred_at | from_location, to_location | performed_by_id | reason
    %%section entity: Instrument; name: Instrument; help: The piece of equipment, its asset tag, where it stands, and whether it can be booked.; rows: name, asset_tag | instrument_type, location | is_active
    %%section entity: Instrument; name: Calibration; help: Whether the instrument is in calibration; results from an out-of-calibration run are not accepted.; rows: calibration_status | last_calibration_date, next_calibration_date
    %%section entity: InstrumentBooking; name: Booking; help: Who reserved which instrument, for which experiment, and when.; rows: instrument_id, experiment_id | booked_by_id, status | start_time, end_time
    %%section entity: InstrumentBooking; name: Cancellation; help: Why the booking was given up, so the slot's history explains the gap.; rows: cancellation_reason; collapsed: true
    %%section entity: Vendor; name: Vendor; help: The outside lab or supplier, what it provides, and whether procurement has approved it.; rows: name, vendor_type | sla_tier, is_approved | country
    %%section entity: Vendor; name: Contact; help: Who to reach at the vendor about a request.; rows: contact_email, contact_phone
    %%section entity: VendorRequest; name: Request; help: The work asked of a vendor, for which experiment, by whom, and where it stands.; rows: request_number, status | vendor_id, experiment_id | raised_by_id | description
    %%section entity: VendorRequest; name: Cost and date; help: What the vendor quoted and when the work is needed by.; rows: quoted_cost, required_date
    %%section entity: DeviationReport; name: Deviation; help: Which experiment departed from its procedure, who reported it, how serious it is, and where it stands.; rows: experiment_id, reported_by_id | severity, status
    %%section entity: DeviationReport; name: What happened; help: The departure itself, when it occurred, and whether equipment failure played a part.; rows: anomaly_timestamp, instrument_failure_involved | anomaly_description
    %%section entity: CAPA; name: Corrective action; help: The action raised against a deviation, who owns it, and where it stands.; rows: title | deviation_report_id, remediation_owner_id | required_resolution_date
    %%section entity: CAPA; name: Root cause and plan; help: Why the deviation happened, what will be done about it, and how success will be measured.; rows: root_cause_category, effectiveness_metric | remediation_plan
    %%section entity: User; name: Person; help: Who the user is and how colleagues reach them.; rows: first_name, last_name | email, role
    %%section entity: User; name: Organisation; help: The team and department the user works in, and whether they can still sign in.; rows: team_id, department | is_active, last_login
    %%section entity: Team; name: Team; help: The research group, its lead, and the department it sits in.; rows: name, manager_id | department

%% ---- Section 2: Business rule — experiment submission gate -----------------
%% What an experiment has to have before anybody's time is booked against it.
%% Every branch here is a refusal a reviewer would otherwise have to make by
%% hand, and each one names the field that is missing rather than saying no.
%%meta name: Experiment Submission Gate
%%meta kind: rules
%%rule experimentSubmitGate on Experiment event: beforeUpdate priority: 10
flowchart TD
    A([Start: Submit Requested]) --> B{Status == draft?}
    B -->|No| X1[Reject: Not in Draft]
    B -->|Yes| C{Title provided?}
    C -->|No| X2[Reject: Title Required]
    C -->|Yes| D{PI assigned?}
    D -->|No| X3[Reject: PI Required]
    D -->|Yes| E{Estimated duration >= 1?}
    E -->|No| X4[Reject: Duration Required]
    E -->|Yes| F{Programme assigned?}
    F -->|No| X5[Reject: Programme Required]
    F -->|Yes| P[Set status: submitted]
    X1 --> Z([End])
    X2 --> Z
    X3 --> Z
    X4 --> Z
    X5 --> Z
    P --> Z

%% ---- Section 3: Business rule — deviation severity routing -----------------
%% Severity is decided, not chosen. A reporter under pressure grades their own
%% mistake gently, so the grade is computed from the context — whether the work
%% was GMP-critical, whether equipment failed, how much was written down.
%%meta name: Deviation Severity Routing
%%meta kind: rules
%%rule deviationSeverity on DeviationReport event: beforeCreate priority: 10
flowchart TD
    A([Start: Deviation Reported]) --> B{GMP-critical experiment?}
    B -->|Yes| C{anomaly_description length >= 100?}
    C -->|Yes| H[Set severity: critical]
    C -->|No| M[Set severity: major]
    B -->|No| D{Instrument failure involved?}
    D -->|Yes| M2[Set severity: major]
    D -->|No| L[Set severity: minor]
    H --> N((Notify QA))
    M --> N
    M2 --> N
    L --> K((Notify Supervisor))
    N --> E([End: Routed])
    K --> E

    %% A critical deviation escalates: the action below names the multi-step
    %% workflow in Section 11, and its `when` is what keeps that workflow from
    %% running on every deviation.
    %%action escalateCritical trigger-workflow when: severity == "critical" workflow: CriticalDeviationEscalation message: Critical deviation — escalating and opening a CAPA

%% ---- Section 4: Business rule — instrument booking conflict check ----------
%% An overdue calibration is a refusal rather than a warning: data taken on an
%% uncalibrated instrument cannot be used, so letting the booking through only
%% wastes the slot and the sample.
%%meta name: Instrument Booking Conflict
%%meta kind: rules
%%rule bookingConflict on InstrumentBooking event: beforeCreate priority: 5
flowchart TD
    A([Start: Booking Requested]) --> B{Instrument is_active?}
    B -->|No| X1[Reject: Instrument Inactive]
    B -->|Yes| C{calibration_status == overdue?}
    C -->|Yes| X2[Reject: Calibration Overdue]
    C -->|No| D{Timeslot available?}
    D -->|No| X3[Reject: Booking Conflict]
    D -->|Yes| P[Confirm Booking]
    X1 --> Z([End])
    X2 --> Z
    X3 --> Z
    P --> Z

%% ---- Section 5: Workflow — programme lifecycle (state machine) --------------
%% A programme advances one stage at a time and can stop at any of them. The
%% edges that are missing are the point: there is no route from hit
%% identification straight to candidate selection, because skipping lead
%% optimization is not a decision anyone is allowed to take by editing a field.
%%meta name: Programme Lifecycle
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow ProgramLifecycle entity: Program kind: state
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> proposed
    proposed     --> active       : fund
    active       --> on_hold      : pause
    on_hold      --> active       : resume
    active       --> completed    : nominate_candidate
    active       --> discontinued : discontinue
    on_hold      --> discontinued : discontinue
    completed    --> [*]
    discontinued --> [*]

    %%trigger webhook:portfolio -> syncProgramStage on Program

%% ---- Section 6: Workflow — experiment lifecycle (state machine) -------------
%%meta name: Experiment Lifecycle
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow ExperimentLifecycle entity: Experiment kind: state
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> draft
    draft        --> submitted   : submit
    submitted    --> in_review   : assign_reviewer
    submitted    --> draft       : return_to_draft
    in_review    --> approved    : approve
    in_review    --> draft       : reject
    approved     --> in_progress : start
    in_progress  --> completed   : complete
    in_progress  --> on_hold     : pause
    on_hold      --> in_progress : resume
    in_progress  --> cancelled   : cancel
    approved     --> cancelled   : cancel
    completed    --> [*]
    cancelled    --> [*]

    %%trigger webhook:lims -> syncExperimentStatus on Experiment

%% ---- Section 7: Workflow — CAPA lifecycle (state machine) ------------------
%%meta name: CAPA Lifecycle
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow CapaLifecycle entity: CAPA kind: state
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> open
    open                 --> in_progress          : assign
    in_progress          --> pending_verification : submit_for_review
    pending_verification --> closed               : verify_effective
    pending_verification --> in_progress          : reopen
    in_progress          --> cancelled            : cancel
    closed               --> [*]
    cancelled            --> [*]


%% ---- The reporting pass (llms.txt §1.1.7) ----------------------------------
%%
%% What the derived pack already gives this model without being asked: a
%% register per entity, a breakdown per %%enum-bound column, created-per-month,
%% a lifecycle report over all three state machines in the diagram's own order,
%% and children-per-parent for all 31 relationships. None of that needed anyone
%% who has run a discovery programme.
%%
%% These do. Each of the eight roles on the %%rbac matrix above is walked
%% through the four questions — what they open first, what tells them something
%% is wrong today, what they are asked for weekly, and what rots silently if
%% nobody looks — so each can do its whole job from what it can see.

%% -- project_manager: the portfolio, and what it is costing -------------------
    %%report pm-programmes-past-candidate-date title: Programmes past their candidate date entity: Program help: The slip nobody announces. A programme still active after the date it was meant to deliver a candidate has silently rebaselined itself, and the portfolio review is where that should surface, not the year end. sql: SELECT p.program_code, p.name AS programme, p.stage, p.therapeutic_area, p.target_candidate_date, (now()::date - p.target_candidate_date) AS days_over FROM bus_program p WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' AND p.target_candidate_date < now()::date ORDER BY days_over DESC
    %%report pm-vendor-commitment title: Committed spend by vendor entity: Vendor chart: bar x: vendor y: committed help: What has been promised to outside parties but not yet delivered. Budget holders ask for this monthly and it exists nowhere else — a purchase order system knows the money, only this knows which experiment it belongs to. sql: SELECT v.name AS vendor, v.vendor_type, v.sla_tier, SUM(r.quoted_cost) AS committed, COUNT(*) AS requests FROM bus_vendor_request r JOIN bus_vendor v ON v.id = r.vendor_id WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND v.deleted_at IS NULL AND r.status IN ('submitted', 'under_review', 'approved', 'in_progress') GROUP BY v.id, v.name, v.vendor_type, v.sla_tier ORDER BY committed DESC
    %%report pm-vendor-requests-stalled title: Vendor requests nobody has moved entity: VendorRequest help: A request sitting in review for a fortnight is an experiment that has quietly stopped. The scientist who raised it has usually moved on to something else and will not chase it. sql: SELECT r.request_number, v.name AS vendor, r.description, r.quoted_cost, r.required_date, r.status, EXTRACT(day FROM now() - r.updated_at)::int AS days_idle FROM bus_vendor_request r JOIN bus_vendor v ON v.id = r.vendor_id WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND r.status IN ('submitted', 'under_review') AND r.updated_at < now() - interval '14 days' ORDER BY days_idle DESC
    %%report pm-series-productivity title: Compounds made per series entity: CompoundSeries chart: bar x: series y: compounds help: Where the chemistry effort actually went last quarter, against where the plan said it would go. An active series with almost no compounds is a hypothesis nobody is testing. sql: SELECT s.series_code || ' ' || s.name AS series, p.name AS programme, s.status, COUNT(c.id) AS compounds FROM bus_compound_series s LEFT JOIN bus_program p ON p.id = s.program_id LEFT JOIN bus_compound c ON c.compound_series_id = s.id AND c.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('exploratory', 'active') GROUP BY s.id, s.series_code, s.name, p.name, s.status ORDER BY compounds DESC

%% -- medicinal_chemist: the molecules they are designing ---------------------
    %%report chem-compounds-awaiting-registration title: Compounds stuck before registration entity: Compound help: A compound that is drawn but not registered cannot be ordered, assayed or cited. It is the commonest bottleneck in the chain and it is invisible unless somebody counts. sql: SELECT c.compound_number, c.common_name, s.name AS series, c.registration_status, EXTRACT(day FROM now() - c.created_at)::int AS days_waiting FROM bus_compound c LEFT JOIN bus_compound_series s ON s.id = c.compound_series_id WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.registration_status IN ('draft', 'pending_review') AND c.created_at < now() - interval '7 days' ORDER BY days_waiting DESC
    %%report chem-rule-of-five-violations title: Compounds outside the rule of five entity: Compound chart: bar x: series y: violators help: Not a rule and not a refusal, but the flag a chemist wants before committing a series to in vivo work. Two or more violations is where oral exposure usually stops being likely. sql: SELECT COALESCE(s.name, '(no series)') AS series, COUNT(*) AS violators, ROUND(AVG(c.molecular_weight)) AS mean_mw, ROUND(AVG(c.logp), 2) AS mean_logp FROM bus_compound c LEFT JOIN bus_compound_series s ON s.id = c.compound_series_id WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.lipinski_violations >= 2 GROUP BY s.id, s.name ORDER BY violators DESC
    %%report chem-most-potent-per-series title: Best validated potency in each series entity: AssayResult help: The number that decides whether a series continues. Validated results only, because an unvalidated hit has broken more programmes than it has started. sql: SELECT s.name AS series, c.compound_number, a.name AS assay, a.readout, MIN(r.value) AS best_value, r.unit FROM bus_assay_result r JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = r.compound_id JOIN bus_assay_run ar ON ar.id = r.assay_run_id JOIN bus_assay a ON a.id = ar.assay_id LEFT JOIN bus_compound_series s ON s.id = c.compound_series_id WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND r.is_validated AND r.qualifier = 'exact' AND a.readout IN ('ic50', 'ec50', 'ki', 'kd') GROUP BY s.name, c.compound_number, a.name, a.readout, r.unit ORDER BY series, best_value
    %%report chem-compounds-never-assayed title: Registered compounds nobody has tested entity: Compound help: Made, approved, and then forgotten. Every one is synthetic effort already spent that is returning nothing, and it is only visible by looking for the absence of a result. sql: SELECT c.compound_number, c.common_name, s.name AS series, c.registration_status, c.created_at FROM bus_compound c LEFT JOIN bus_compound_series s ON s.id = c.compound_series_id LEFT JOIN bus_assay_result r ON r.compound_id = c.id AND r.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.registration_status = 'approved' GROUP BY c.id, c.compound_number, c.common_name, s.name, c.registration_status, c.created_at HAVING COUNT(r.id) = 0 ORDER BY c.created_at

%% -- biologist: the assays and what came off them ----------------------------
    %%report bio-runs-failing-qc title: Assay runs that failed QC entity: AssayRun help: Checked before any result from the same plate is believed. A failed run whose results were already read into a series decision is the most expensive mistake in this application. sql: SELECT ar.run_reference, a.name AS assay, ar.run_date, ar.plate_barcode, ar.z_prime, ar.qc_status, ar.failure_reason FROM bus_assay_run ar JOIN bus_assay a ON a.id = ar.assay_id WHERE ar.deleted_at IS NULL AND ar.qc_status IN ('fail', 'flagged') AND ar.run_date > now()::date - 60 ORDER BY ar.run_date DESC
    %%report bio-runs-below-z-prime title: Runs below the assay's own Z' threshold entity: AssayRun help: The quieter QC failure: a run marked pass whose Z' is under the acceptance figure the assay itself declares. The comparison is per assay, which is why no fixed threshold would find these. sql: SELECT ar.run_reference, a.name AS assay, a.acceptance_z_prime AS required, ar.z_prime AS actual, ar.run_date, ar.qc_status FROM bus_assay_run ar JOIN bus_assay a ON a.id = ar.assay_id WHERE ar.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.acceptance_z_prime IS NOT NULL AND ar.z_prime IS NOT NULL AND ar.z_prime < a.acceptance_z_prime ORDER BY (a.acceptance_z_prime - ar.z_prime) DESC
    %%report bio-unvalidated-results title: Results nobody has signed off entity: AssayResult help: An unvalidated result is a number that will be quoted anyway. This is the queue that keeps that from happening, and it is nobody's job unless it is on a screen. sql: SELECT a.name AS assay, c.compound_number, r.value, r.unit, r.qualifier, r.replicate_count, ar.run_date FROM bus_assay_result r JOIN bus_assay_run ar ON ar.id = r.assay_run_id JOIN bus_assay a ON a.id = ar.assay_id JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = r.compound_id WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND NOT r.is_validated AND ar.status = 'completed' ORDER BY ar.run_date
    %%report bio-samples-expiring title: Samples about to expire entity: Sample help: Material that will have to be remade if nobody uses it in time. Remaking a sample costs a chemist a week, and the expiry date is on a screen nobody opens. sql: SELECT s.sample_barcode, c.compound_number, s.physical_state, s.volume_ul, s.storage_location, s.expiration_date, (s.expiration_date - now()::date) AS days_left FROM bus_sample s LEFT JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = s.compound_id WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('available', 'reserved') AND s.expiration_date BETWEEN now()::date AND (now() + interval '45 days')::date ORDER BY s.expiration_date
    %%report bio-runs-retested-repeatedly title: Assays being run again and again entity: AssayRun chart: bar x: assay y: retests help: A retest count climbing on one assay is a protocol that has stopped working, not a run of bad luck. It is visible only when the runs are counted together. sql: SELECT a.name AS assay, a.assay_type, SUM(ar.retest_count) AS retests, COUNT(*) AS runs FROM bus_assay_run ar JOIN bus_assay a ON a.id = ar.assay_id WHERE ar.deleted_at IS NULL AND ar.retest_count > 0 AND ar.run_date > now()::date - 90 GROUP BY a.id, a.name, a.assay_type ORDER BY retests DESC

%% -- computational_chemist: the property space -------------------------------
    %%report comp-compounds-without-admet title: Compounds with no ADMET profile entity: Compound help: A compound advancing on potency alone. The modelling team is asked to explain a late failure that an early profile would have predicted, and this is the list of the ones that could still be profiled cheaply. sql: SELECT c.compound_number, c.common_name, s.name AS series, c.molecular_weight, c.logp FROM bus_compound c LEFT JOIN bus_compound_series s ON s.id = c.compound_series_id LEFT JOIN bus_admet_profile p ON p.compound_id = c.id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.registration_status = 'approved' GROUP BY c.id, c.compound_number, c.common_name, s.name, c.molecular_weight, c.logp HAVING COUNT(p.id) = 0 ORDER BY c.compound_number
    %%report comp-property-space-by-series title: Where each series sits in property space entity: CompoundSeries chart: bar x: series y: mean_logp help: Mean physicochemistry per series, which is how a series is recognised as drifting greasy long before any assay says so. Drift here precedes solubility and hERG problems by months. sql: SELECT s.name AS series, COUNT(c.id) AS compounds, ROUND(AVG(c.molecular_weight)) AS mean_mw, ROUND(AVG(c.logp), 2) AS mean_logp, ROUND(AVG(c.tpsa)) AS mean_tpsa FROM bus_compound_series s JOIN bus_compound c ON c.compound_series_id = s.id AND c.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('exploratory', 'active') GROUP BY s.id, s.name HAVING COUNT(c.id) > 0 ORDER BY mean_logp DESC
    %%report comp-admet-liabilities title: Compounds carrying an ADMET liability entity: AdmetProfile help: One row per compound that a modeller would flag before it is proposed for in vivo work: a positive Ames, a hERG margin that is too narrow, or solubility low enough to make every later result doubtful. sql: SELECT c.compound_number, p.ames_result, p.herg_ic50_um, p.kinetic_solubility_um, p.microsomal_half_life_min, p.assessed_on FROM bus_admet_profile p JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = p.compound_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND (p.ames_result = 'positive' OR p.herg_ic50_um < 10 OR p.kinetic_solubility_um < 10) ORDER BY p.assessed_on DESC

%% -- dmpk_scientist: what the body will do to the molecule -------------------
    %%report dmpk-herg-risk title: Compounds with a narrow hERG margin entity: AdmetProfile chart: bar x: compound y: herg help: The cardiac liability that stops programmes at the last gate. Under ten micromolar is where a DMPK scientist wants the conversation to happen — while there is still a series to change. sql: SELECT c.compound_number AS compound, p.herg_ic50_um AS herg, p.plasma_protein_bound_percent, p.assessed_on FROM bus_admet_profile p JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = p.compound_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.herg_ic50_um IS NOT NULL AND p.herg_ic50_um < 10 ORDER BY p.herg_ic50_um
    %%report dmpk-metabolic-instability title: Compounds cleared too fast to dose entity: AdmetProfile help: A microsomal half-life under thirty minutes means the compound is gone before it works, whatever the potency says. Paired with CYP inhibition because the two together decide whether a series is dosable at all. sql: SELECT c.compound_number, p.microsomal_half_life_min, p.cyp3a4_inhibition_percent, p.caco2_permeability, p.assessed_on FROM bus_admet_profile p JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = p.compound_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.microsomal_half_life_min IS NOT NULL AND p.microsomal_half_life_min < 30 ORDER BY p.microsomal_half_life_min
    %%report dmpk-profiles-going-stale title: ADMET profiles older than the chemistry entity: AdmetProfile help: A profile measured a year ago against a compound the series has since moved past. Nobody re-runs these unless asked, and decisions keep being made on them. sql: SELECT c.compound_number, s.name AS series, p.assessed_on, (now()::date - p.assessed_on) AS days_old FROM bus_admet_profile p JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = p.compound_id LEFT JOIN bus_compound_series s ON s.id = c.compound_series_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.assessed_on < now()::date - 365 ORDER BY p.assessed_on

%% -- lab_manager: the laboratory as a working thing --------------------------
    %%report lab-calibration-overdue title: Instruments overdue for calibration entity: Instrument help: The first thing an auditor asks for, and the thing that invalidates every run made on the instrument since the date passed. Due-soon is included because that is when it is still cheap to book. sql: SELECT i.asset_tag, i.name AS instrument, i.instrument_type, i.location, i.calibration_status, i.next_calibration_date, (now()::date - i.next_calibration_date) AS days_overdue FROM bus_instrument i WHERE i.deleted_at IS NULL AND i.is_active AND i.calibration_status IN ('overdue', 'due_soon') ORDER BY i.next_calibration_date
    %%report lab-instrument-utilisation title: Hours booked on each instrument entity: Instrument chart: bar x: instrument y: hours help: What to buy next and what to retire. An instrument at capacity is a queue everybody is working around; one at nothing is money sitting in a corner of the lab. sql: SELECT i.name AS instrument, i.instrument_type, ROUND(SUM(EXTRACT(epoch FROM (b.end_time - b.start_time)) / 3600)) AS hours, COUNT(*) AS bookings FROM bus_instrument_booking b JOIN bus_instrument i ON i.id = b.instrument_id WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status IN ('confirmed', 'in_use', 'completed') AND b.start_time > now() - interval '90 days' GROUP BY i.id, i.name, i.instrument_type ORDER BY hours DESC
    %%report lab-open-deviations title: Deviations still open entity: DeviationReport help: Every one is an experiment whose result is in question until it is closed. Severity first, because a critical deviation open for a week is a regulatory finding waiting to be written. sql: SELECT d.severity, e.title AS experiment, u.first_name || ' ' || u.last_name AS reported_by, d.status, d.anomaly_timestamp, d.instrument_failure_involved FROM bus_deviation_report d LEFT JOIN bus_experiment e ON e.id = d.experiment_id LEFT JOIN bus_user u ON u.id = d.reported_by_id WHERE d.deleted_at IS NULL AND d.status IN ('open', 'under_investigation', 'escalated') ORDER BY CASE d.severity WHEN 'catastrophic' THEN 1 WHEN 'critical' THEN 2 WHEN 'major' THEN 3 ELSE 4 END, d.anomaly_timestamp
    %%report lab-capa-overdue title: CAPAs past their resolution date entity: CAPA help: A corrective action that has passed its own deadline is the finding, not the deviation that caused it. This is the report that gets read aloud in a quality review. sql: SELECT k.title, k.root_cause_category, k.status, k.required_resolution_date, (now()::date - k.required_resolution_date) AS days_overdue, u.first_name || ' ' || u.last_name AS owner FROM bus_capa k LEFT JOIN bus_user u ON u.id = k.remediation_owner_id WHERE k.deleted_at IS NULL AND k.status NOT IN ('closed', 'cancelled') AND k.required_resolution_date < now()::date ORDER BY days_overdue DESC
    %%report lab-experiments-stalled title: Experiments that have stopped moving entity: Experiment help: Approved, started, and then nothing for a month. The principal investigator has moved on and the reserved samples and instrument time are still allocated to it. sql: SELECT e.project_code, e.title, u.first_name || ' ' || u.last_name AS pi, e.status, e.submitted_at, EXTRACT(day FROM now() - e.updated_at)::int AS days_idle FROM bus_experiment e LEFT JOIN bus_user u ON u.id = e.pi_id WHERE e.deleted_at IS NULL AND e.status IN ('approved', 'in_progress') AND e.updated_at < now() - interval '30 days' ORDER BY days_idle DESC
    %%report lab-samples-quarantined title: Samples in quarantine entity: Sample help: Material nobody may use and nobody has released. It occupies freezer space, it is often the only aliquot of a compound, and it is released only when somebody notices it is there. sql: SELECT s.sample_barcode, c.compound_number, s.storage_location, s.storage_temperature, s.volume_ul, s.mass_mg, s.updated_at FROM bus_sample s LEFT JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = s.compound_id WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'quarantined' ORDER BY s.updated_at

%% -- lab_technician: the bench, today ----------------------------------------
    %%report tech-todays-bookings title: Instrument bookings for today entity: InstrumentBooking help: The bench plan for the day, in start order. A technician needs the instrument, the room it is in, and who booked it, which is three joins and no screen shows all three. sql: SELECT b.start_time, b.end_time, i.name AS instrument, i.location, e.title AS experiment, u.first_name || ' ' || u.last_name AS booked_by, b.status FROM bus_instrument_booking b JOIN bus_instrument i ON i.id = b.instrument_id LEFT JOIN bus_experiment e ON e.id = b.experiment_id LEFT JOIN bus_user u ON u.id = b.booked_by_id WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status IN ('pending', 'confirmed', 'in_use') AND b.start_time >= date_trunc('day', now()) AND b.start_time < date_trunc('day', now()) + interval '1 day' ORDER BY b.start_time
    %%report tech-samples-running-out title: Samples nearly used up entity: Sample help: The aliquot that will not cover the next plate. Found at the bench in the middle of a run, or found here the day before, and only one of those costs a repeat. sql: SELECT s.sample_barcode, c.compound_number, s.volume_ul, s.mass_mg, s.concentration_mm, s.storage_location, s.status FROM bus_sample s LEFT JOIN bus_compound c ON c.id = s.compound_id WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('available', 'reserved') AND ((s.volume_ul IS NOT NULL AND s.volume_ul < 50) OR (s.mass_mg IS NOT NULL AND s.mass_mg < 1)) ORDER BY s.volume_ul NULLS LAST
    %%report tech-runs-to-repeat title: Runs waiting to be repeated entity: AssayRun help: Failed or cancelled runs that nobody has rebooked. The plate is gone, the compound is still on the list, and the biologist is waiting for a number that is not coming. sql: SELECT ar.run_reference, a.name AS assay, i.name AS instrument, ar.run_date, ar.status, ar.failure_reason FROM bus_assay_run ar JOIN bus_assay a ON a.id = ar.assay_id LEFT JOIN bus_instrument i ON i.id = ar.instrument_id WHERE ar.deleted_at IS NULL AND ar.status IN ('failed', 'cancelled') AND ar.run_date > now()::date - 30 ORDER BY ar.run_date DESC

%% -- quality_assurance: what an auditor would ask for ------------------------
    %%report qa-critical-deviations title: Critical and catastrophic deviations entity: DeviationReport help: The set that has to be defensible line by line. Anything here that is still open, or closed without a CAPA, is a finding in the next inspection. sql: SELECT d.severity, d.status, e.title AS experiment, e.is_gmp_critical, d.anomaly_timestamp, d.anomaly_description FROM bus_deviation_report d LEFT JOIN bus_experiment e ON e.id = d.experiment_id WHERE d.deleted_at IS NULL AND d.severity IN ('critical', 'catastrophic') ORDER BY d.anomaly_timestamp DESC
    %%report qa-deviations-without-capa title: Deviations closed with no corrective action entity: DeviationReport help: A deviation resolved without a CAPA is a problem declared solved with nothing recorded about how. Sometimes correct, always worth a question, and never visible from the deviation screen itself. sql: SELECT d.severity, e.title AS experiment, d.status, d.anomaly_timestamp, d.anomaly_description FROM bus_deviation_report d LEFT JOIN bus_experiment e ON e.id = d.experiment_id LEFT JOIN bus_capa k ON k.deviation_report_id = d.id AND k.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE d.deleted_at IS NULL AND d.status IN ('resolved', 'closed') AND d.severity IN ('major', 'critical', 'catastrophic') GROUP BY d.id, d.severity, e.title, d.status, d.anomaly_timestamp, d.anomaly_description HAVING COUNT(k.id) = 0 ORDER BY d.anomaly_timestamp DESC
    %%report qa-capa-effectiveness title: How well corrective actions worked entity: CAPA chart: bar x: cause y: mean_effectiveness help: Closed CAPAs grouped by what caused them, with the effectiveness the model already records. A root cause that keeps recurring with a high score is a measurement that is not measuring anything. sql: SELECT k.root_cause_category AS cause, COUNT(*) AS capas, ROUND(AVG(k.effectiveness_metric), 2) AS mean_effectiveness FROM bus_capa k WHERE k.deleted_at IS NULL AND k.status = 'closed' AND k.effectiveness_metric IS NOT NULL GROUP BY k.root_cause_category ORDER BY capas DESC
    %%report qa-gmp-experiments-at-risk title: GMP-critical experiments carrying deviations entity: Experiment help: The intersection an auditor goes to first: work declared GMP-critical that also has an unresolved deviation against it. Neither screen shows the other, so this is the only place the pair appears. sql: SELECT e.project_code, e.title, e.status, COUNT(d.id) AS open_deviations, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE d.severity IN ('critical', 'catastrophic')) AS critical_or_worse FROM bus_experiment e JOIN bus_deviation_report d ON d.experiment_id = e.id AND d.deleted_at IS NULL AND d.status IN ('open', 'under_investigation', 'escalated') WHERE e.deleted_at IS NULL AND e.is_gmp_critical GROUP BY e.id, e.project_code, e.title, e.status ORDER BY open_deviations DESC
    %%report qa-runs-on-uncalibrated-instruments title: Runs made on instruments that were overdue entity: AssayRun help: The report that justifies the calibration discipline. Every run listed produced numbers that an auditor can challenge, and the challenge is far cheaper to answer before it is made. sql: SELECT ar.run_reference, a.name AS assay, i.asset_tag, i.name AS instrument, i.calibration_status, ar.run_date, ar.qc_status FROM bus_assay_run ar JOIN bus_instrument i ON i.id = ar.instrument_id JOIN bus_assay a ON a.id = ar.assay_id WHERE ar.deleted_at IS NULL AND i.deleted_at IS NULL AND i.calibration_status IN ('overdue', 'under_maintenance') AND ar.run_date > now()::date - 180 ORDER BY ar.run_date DESC
    %%report program-by-status title: Programs by status entity: Program chart: pie x: status y: records help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. How programs divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_program WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report program-by-stage title: Programs by stage entity: Program chart: pie x: stage y: records help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. How programs divide across stage, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(stage::text, '(unset)') AS stage, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_program WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report program-by-therapeutic-area title: Programs by therapeutic area entity: Program chart: bar x: therapeutic_area y: records help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. How programs divide across therapeutic area, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(therapeutic_area::text, '(unset)') AS therapeutic_area, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_program WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report program-lifecycle title: Program lifecycle entity: Program chart: bar x: state y: records help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. Where programs sit in their state machine, in the order the diagram draws it; a state nothing has reached shows as zero. sql: SELECT d.state, COALESCE(c.records, 0) AS records FROM (SELECT 'proposed' AS state, 0 AS position UNION ALL SELECT 'active', 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'on_hold', 2 UNION ALL SELECT 'completed', 3 UNION ALL SELECT 'discontinued', 4) d LEFT JOIN (SELECT status AS state, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_program WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) c ON c.state = d.state ORDER BY d.position
    %%report program-annual-budget-by-status title: Total annual budget by status entity: Program chart: bar x: status y: total_annual_budget help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. Programs added up on annual budget, split by status, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, ROUND(SUM(annual_budget)::numeric, 2) AS total_annual_budget FROM bus_program WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
    %%report program-annual-budget-per-month title: Average annual budget per month entity: Program chart: line x: month y: average_annual_budget help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. The average annual budget of programs created each month, to see whether the typical record is growing or shrinking. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(AVG(annual_budget)::numeric, 2) AS average_annual_budget FROM bus_program WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report program-largest-by-annual-budget title: The ten largest programs by annual budget entity: Program chart: bar x: record y: annual_budget help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. The records with the highest annual budget; worth reading for outliers and for the few that carry most of the total. sql: SELECT name::text AS record, annual_budget FROM bus_program WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND annual_budget IS NOT NULL ORDER BY annual_budget DESC LIMIT 10
    %%report program-per-target title: Programs per target entity: Program chart: bar x: target y: records help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. Which target the most programs belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS target, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_program x LEFT JOIN bus_target p ON p.id = x.target_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
    %%report program-annual-budget-per-target title: Total annual budget per target entity: Program chart: bar x: target y: total_annual_budget help: A funded attempt to find a medicine for one indication by acting on one biological target. Programs added up on annual budget for each target, ten largest first. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS target, ROUND(SUM(x.annual_budget)::numeric, 2) AS total_annual_budget FROM bus_program x LEFT JOIN bus_target p ON p.id = x.target_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 10
    %%report target-by-target-class title: Targets by target class entity: Target chart: bar x: target_class y: records help: A protein or gene the programme intends to act on. How targets divide across target class, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(target_class::text, '(unset)') AS target_class, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_target WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report target-by-validation-level title: Targets by validation level entity: Target chart: pie x: validation_level y: records help: A protein or gene the programme intends to act on. How targets divide across validation level, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(validation_level::text, '(unset)') AS validation_level, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_target WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report target-created-per-month title: Targets created per month entity: Target chart: line x: month y: records help: A protein or gene the programme intends to act on. New targets per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_target WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report compound-series-by-status title: Compound Serieses by status entity: CompoundSeries chart: pie x: status y: records help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. How compound serieses divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_compound_series WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report compound-series-compound-count-by-status title: Total compound count by status entity: CompoundSeries chart: bar x: status y: total_compound_count help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. Compound Serieses added up on compound count, split by status, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, ROUND(SUM(compound_count)::numeric, 2) AS total_compound_count FROM bus_compound_series WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
    %%report compound-series-compound-count-per-month title: Average compound count per month entity: CompoundSeries chart: line x: month y: average_compound_count help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. The average compound count of compound serieses created each month, to see whether the typical record is growing or shrinking. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(AVG(compound_count)::numeric, 2) AS average_compound_count FROM bus_compound_series WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report compound-series-largest-by-compound-count title: The ten largest compound serieses by compound count entity: CompoundSeries chart: bar x: record y: compound_count help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. The records with the highest compound count; worth reading for outliers and for the few that carry most of the total. sql: SELECT name::text AS record, compound_count FROM bus_compound_series WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND compound_count IS NOT NULL ORDER BY compound_count DESC LIMIT 10
    %%report compound-series-per-program title: Compound Serieses per program entity: CompoundSeries chart: bar x: program y: records help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. Which program the most compound serieses belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS program, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_compound_series x LEFT JOIN bus_program p ON p.id = x.program_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
    %%report compound-series-compound-count-per-program title: Total compound count per program entity: CompoundSeries chart: bar x: program y: total_compound_count help: A family of compounds sharing a chemical scaffold, explored together. Compound Serieses added up on compound count for each program, ten largest first. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS program, ROUND(SUM(x.compound_count)::numeric, 2) AS total_compound_count FROM bus_compound_series x LEFT JOIN bus_program p ON p.id = x.program_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 10
    %%report compound-by-compound-class title: Compounds by compound class entity: Compound chart: pie x: compound_class y: records help: One registered chemical structure. How compounds divide across compound class, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(compound_class::text, '(unset)') AS compound_class, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_compound WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
